course: dictionary
lesson: 10202
title: Tiny: very, very small
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Tiny means very, very small. Picture «a tiny ant»: an ant is already small, and a tiny ant is smaller still.
explain: Context helps you check the meaning. In science, tiny describes germs and cells, which you can only see with a microscope. In maths, it can describe tiny amounts or numbers. Ask: what word would fit here? Then try "very small" in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: In stories, tiny can make something seem cute or fragile. In «tiny words like "please" and "thank you"», the words are small in size but big in how they make people feel. Phrases like «a tiny bit» and «tiny little» keep the same meaning: very small.
fact: Tiny means very, very small.
fact: Tiny is an adjective, and its forms are tiny, tinier and tiniest.
fact: «Bubbles start at tiny places like scratches or specks.»
fact: «Tiny bit» means a very small amount.
fact: In science, germs and cells are so tiny that you need a microscope to see them.
exercise: What does tiny mean in «a tiny ant»? | *Very, very small | Very loud | Very fast | Very old
exercise: «Water with impurities (tiny bits of other things mixed in it) can conduct electricity.» What are the tiny bits? | Bright colours | *Very small pieces | Cold drinks | Huge lumps
exercise: Which phrase means a very small amount? | Very tall | Bright light | *Tiny bit | Huge heap
test: What is the comparative form of tiny? | *Tinier | Tinyer | Tiniest | More tiny
test: «The same lens can gather sunlight into a tiny burning spot.» What size is the burning spot? | Very wet | Very cold | *Very small | Very large
exam: Which sentence uses tiny correctly? | The elephant was tiny and filled the whole room. | *Germs are so tiny that you need a microscope to see them. | The desert stretched tiny for miles across the sand. | The mountain was tiny and towered over the clouds.
